        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "In SLES-based formulations, viscosity can be increased simply by adding sodium chloride (NaCl) electrolyte, which compresses the electrical double layer of the anionic surfactant head groups, promoting micellar growth into rod-like structures that increase solution viscosity at low concentrations. For more precise rheology control and formulations with mixed or nonionic-heavy surfactant systems, polymeric thickeners such as hydroxyethylcellulose (HEC), xanthan gum, or acrylate-based carbomers are used. Thickener choice also determines whether the product has a clear or opaque appearance and how it pours and sprays from a trigger bottle."
